# Line-ending policy.
#
# The eval gold sets are INTEGRITY-PINNED: each labels.json records the sha256
# of its fixtures/corpus, and the eval refuses to run on a mismatch (that check
# is the point — a silently edited gold set would make every quality number a
# lie). The recorded hashes were computed over LF content.
#
# With core.autocrlf=true and no policy here, Windows checks these files out
# with CRLF. Every byte of every line ending changes, so the on-disk sha256
# never matches the recorded one and the gold-set checks fail before they test
# anything — on the platform this repo is primarily developed on (see
# CLAUDE.md: "developed on Windows under Git Bash"). Measured: the reviewer
# fixtures hash b74d7500… as stored by git and ebb8aa9d… as checked out here,
# and labels.json expects b74d7500…
#
# So: data that is hashed must be byte-identical on every platform. `eol=lf`
# pins the checkout, and `text` keeps git normalizing on commit so a file
# edited on Windows cannot re-introduce CRLF into the blob.
eval/**/*.json text eol=lf

# Shell scripts must stay LF regardless of platform — the gate, the pipeline
# and every adapter run under bash, and a CRLF shebang line is not executable.
*.sh text eol=lf
